WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:09.160
Greetings, fellow travelers.

2
00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:12.820
It is I, Rich Roll, once again, after a brief respite,

3
00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:15.170
the nature of which will soon be clear,

4
00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:18.140
here in brotherhood, with my podcast husband,

5
00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:22.090 line:15%
Mr. Adam Skolnick, master of pen, scroll and word.

6
00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:26.330 line:15%
Here to pontificate, to traverse a diversity of terrain,

7
00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:29.890 line:15%
both high-minded and perhaps frivolous.

8
00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:32.840
For those new to the roll-on edition of the podcast,

9
00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:35.300
this is all about stories, big and small,

10
00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:36.580
what catches our attention,

11
00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:38.900
what deserves a brighter spotlight.

12
00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:41.440
We typically buttress that with a bit of show and tell.

13
00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:44.760
We share a few wins of the week and we round it all out

14
00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:47.090
by answering some of the questions dropped

15
00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:49.000
on our voicemail, which you can ring up

16
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:50.150
at 4-2-4-2-3-5-4-6-2-6.

17
00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:54.970
What is going on, my man?

18
00:00:54.970 --> 00:00:56.410
Just sitting here contemplating

19
00:00:56.410 --> 00:00:58.963
the magnificent interdependence of all things.

20
00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:03.550
Well, that should be chalked up as frivolous.

21
00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:05.038
Oh, it's supposed to be frivolous?

22
00:01:05.038 --> 00:01:05.871
Yeah.

23
00:01:05.871 --> 00:01:07.970
The way I approach it is very frivolous though.

24
00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:09.660
I approach it like--
On the surface level?

25
00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:11.740
Yeah, like, all the people that can

26
00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:14.210
end up working towards giving me my snacks.

27
00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:16.140
Listen, a little existential angst

28
00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:19.040
is good fuel for the fire.

29
00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:21.630
But I don't think it's angst, you know,

30
00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:26.120
maybe it's fatherhood or maybe it's insomnia.

31
00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:31.120
But I have been focused on the great mesh of society

32
00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:34.920
and how so many people play their role,

33
00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:36.560
and in so doing, you know,

34
00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:39.970
society works and it's kind of phrased sometimes,

35
00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:42.467
and there's like little holes that we have to patch up.

36
00:01:42.467 --> 00:01:46.470
But for the most part, we are so interdependent

37
00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:47.446
we don't even think about it.

38
00:01:47.446 --> 00:01:49.700
We don't really take time to think about

39
00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:52.290
how much others do for us and we do for others,

40
00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:53.950
just by playing our role.

41
00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:55.540
I think it's a beautiful thing.

42
00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:57.930
Did you do DMT over the weekend?

43
00:01:57.930 --> 00:01:59.857
I wish, I wish.

44
00:01:59.857 --> 00:02:02.820
We are but cogs in the wheel of a matrix

45
00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:06.690
that although perhaps flawed at times does cohere.

46
00:02:06.690 --> 00:02:08.020
It coheres.

47
00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:11.044
It feels coherent at most of the time, even...

48
00:02:11.044 --> 00:02:15.540
We're tiny little cells in the microbiome

49
00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:18.180
of a greater ecosystem.

50
00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:20.030
We play our small little part

51
00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:23.280
with being blindly unaware of the greater mechanisms

52
00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:24.113
at play.

53
00:02:24.113 --> 00:02:25.680
Right, and occasionally things fail.

54
00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:30.010
Like when the sewage system of Los Angeles failed

55
00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:33.330
for like seven hours and 17 million gallons

56
00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:37.030
of raw sewage spilled out around DockWeiler Beach

57
00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:38.230
in the Santa Monica bay.

58
00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:41.850
It fails sometimes, that is not a criticism

59
00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:44.020
of the good people of the sewage treatment plant.

60
00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:45.810
It just happened that it was a failure.

61
00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:47.034
Shit goes sideways.

62
00:02:47.034 --> 00:02:48.277
Literally shit went sideways
It is weird how,

63
00:02:48.277 --> 00:02:50.260
here, everybody knows

64
00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:53.330
that for a period of, I dunno, you might know better,

65
00:02:53.330 --> 00:02:55.440
how many days after there's a rainstorm

66
00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:56.900
that you just don't go in the ocean

67
00:02:56.900 --> 00:02:58.560
and we all have just normalized that,

68
00:02:58.560 --> 00:02:59.620
like, okay, you don't go in.

69
00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:01.130
And then at some point we decide,

70
00:03:01.130 --> 00:03:02.580
I guess it's okay to go back in.

71
00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:06.830
Right, well now, there is a way to figure it out.

72
00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:08.400
You can go on the LA county website,

73
00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:10.720
but after this, they were really taking a lot of tests.

74
00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:14.210
And so now everything is good except for like one beach.

75
00:03:14.210 --> 00:03:15.043
So we're back in.

76
00:03:15.043 --> 00:03:16.040
I got back in on Saturday,

77
00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:18.090
I was like a week out of the water.

78
00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:19.640
Got the gills wet again.

79
00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:22.600
It was fun, but everything's good.

80
00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:24.340
We have a family of red tailed hawks

81
00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:25.860
in the yard, that live in our yard,

82
00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:26.693
which is pretty cool.

83
00:03:26.693 --> 00:03:27.801
That's cool.

84
00:03:27.801 --> 00:03:29.270
You've moved into a new abode.

85
00:03:29.270 --> 00:03:31.390
We moved into the hawk's nest.

86
00:03:31.390 --> 00:03:33.020
I guess that's what I'm gonna name it maybe.

87
00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:35.010
That's cool.

88
00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:38.430
On that subject of being cells in a greater

89
00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:40.283
Yes.
Macro organism.

90
00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:42.810
That really gets hammered home

91
00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:45.810
when you are in a plane and you're descending

92
00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:48.800
into Los Angeles and you just see the grid.

93
00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:50.290
I agree.
It just goes forever.

94
00:03:50.290 --> 00:03:53.370
And all the cars on the freeway that are like blood cells,

95
00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:56.220
delivering oxygen to the various organs

96
00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:59.980
so that it can all work as a greater system.

97
00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:02.410
And it makes you think on some level,

98
00:04:02.410 --> 00:04:06.950
we are just unconsciously participating

99
00:04:06.950 --> 00:04:08.510
in some grander play,

100
00:04:08.510 --> 00:04:10.440
where we play this tiny little role

101
00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:12.190
that allows the whole thing to function.

102
00:04:12.190 --> 00:04:13.410
And it's a trip, man.

103
00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:14.243
It is a trip.

104
00:04:14.243 --> 00:04:16.610
We're all the stars of our own great grand opera

105
00:04:16.610 --> 00:04:18.041
that no one else is watching.

106
00:04:18.041 --> 00:04:19.020
(laughs)

107
00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:20.680
How do you know?

108
00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:22.820
Somebody very well maybe watching.

109
00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:26.320
Maybe, but also the honeycomb that is New York,

110
00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:27.210
kind of the same thing.

111
00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:29.293
You go, you drop in,

112
00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:32.330
that's when you can feel small in the city.

113
00:04:32.330 --> 00:04:33.990
You know, often for me,

114
00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:36.610
I like to get out into nature to feel the reductionist.

115
00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:39.320
But that's some of the beautiful things about New York.

116
00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:41.860
One of the many things that's so exciting about New York

117
00:04:41.860 --> 00:04:44.300
is that you feel so small in this amazing,

118
00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:46.240
like hive of activity.

119
00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:49.360
I feel very at home there and I feel very alive.

120
00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:52.170
And also, I don't necessarily feel small.

121
00:04:52.170 --> 00:04:55.160
I feel much more plugged in than I do here in Los Angeles.

122
00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:58.320
So on the metaphor of the hive and the bee,

123
00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:00.880
am I taking pollen from Los Angeles

124
00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:02.820
and pollinating New York?

125
00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:04.600
Or is it vice versa?

126
00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:05.433
I don't know.

127
00:05:05.433 --> 00:05:07.720
There's some sort of cross pollination happening.

128
00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:09.120
You're cross pollinating friend.

129
00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:11.550
Yeah, but I did spend nine days in New York,

130
00:05:11.550 --> 00:05:13.960
which is why we had to take a bit of a respite

131
00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:16.770
from roll-on, because I was out of town.

132
00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:20.890
It's my first trip back there since before the pandemic,

133
00:05:20.890 --> 00:05:24.830
I think I visited shortly before everything locked down

134
00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:27.463
and it was freaking awesome to be there.

135
00:05:27.463 --> 00:05:31.253
I just get such an energy boost from being in the city.

136
00:05:32.395 --> 00:05:36.350
Despite what people may be hearing about law and order

137
00:05:36.350 --> 00:05:38.850
and about everything being shut down

138
00:05:38.850 --> 00:05:42.420
and it being a city, monopolized by fear.

139
00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:43.253
My experience was very different.

140
00:05:43.253 --> 00:05:45.217
I mean, I was in lower Manhattan.

141
00:05:45.217 --> 00:05:47.130
You know, if you go to Midtown,

142
00:05:47.130 --> 00:05:48.790
there's a lot of stuff that's shut down

143
00:05:48.790 --> 00:05:51.170
and who knows what's going on in all those office buildings.

144
00:05:51.170 --> 00:05:54.110
But Downtown, there's just so much young energy,

145
00:05:54.110 --> 00:05:55.670
like everybody's out on the street.

146
00:05:55.670 --> 00:06:00.340
And all the cafes have created these outdoor dining

147
00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:03.900
situations that, by all accounts,

148
00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:05.750
look like they're gonna be permanent,

149
00:06:05.750 --> 00:06:07.330
which has made a lot of the streets,

150
00:06:07.330 --> 00:06:09.400
much more walkable and much more bikeable.

151
00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:12.330
A lot of the streets on the lower east side

152
00:06:12.330 --> 00:06:14.800
are actually cordoned off to traffic.

153
00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:15.700
Is that where you were, the lower east?

154
00:06:15.700 --> 00:06:18.000
Yeah, I stayed on the lower east side.

155
00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:20.150
I had a bike that I could ride around,

156
00:06:20.150 --> 00:06:21.470
which was awesome.

157
00:06:21.470 --> 00:06:23.340
And it was so much fun.

158
00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:26.870
And what's great about New York is all the synchronicities

159
00:06:26.870 --> 00:06:29.850
that you put yourself in a position to experience.

160
00:06:29.850 --> 00:06:33.150
And my history with New York is that,

161
00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:36.560
there is no other place on earth where that happens

162
00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:39.020
with such frequency.

163
00:06:39.020 --> 00:06:40.740
So people know Davey Greenberg

164
00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:43.460
who shoots photographs for us.

165
00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:45.640
I ran into Davy's brother who I'd never met.

166
00:06:45.640 --> 00:06:47.850
I just knew what he looked like from Davey's Instagram.

167
00:06:47.850 --> 00:06:48.683
Oh really?

168
00:06:48.683 --> 00:06:49.647
You didn't plan that?

169
00:06:49.647 --> 00:06:51.100
No, I just saw him and I was like,

170
00:06:51.100 --> 00:06:52.740
hey Nile, you know, what's up.

171
00:06:52.740 --> 00:06:55.140
And then I ran into Davey's girlfriend,

172
00:06:55.140 --> 00:06:57.920
who was like staying in the same hotel as me.

173
00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:00.020
I mean, it was wild.

174
00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:04.050
It's just so fun to be in the flow of life there.

175
00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:05.410
Everything is so accessible.

176
00:07:05.410 --> 00:07:07.010
Everything's five to 10 minutes away

177
00:07:07.010 --> 00:07:08.560
and you can get so much done.

178
00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:10.900
I just felt this resurgence of life,

179
00:07:10.900 --> 00:07:13.760
I felt like myself again, which was really cool.

180
00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:16.240
Where does Rich Roll stay in New York City?

181
00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:18.950
I stayed at the public hotel.

182
00:07:18.950 --> 00:07:20.180
I'd never stayed there before,

183
00:07:20.180 --> 00:07:23.500
but it's pretty centrally located like right in the Bowery.

184
00:07:23.500 --> 00:07:25.390
Cool.
It's pretty great.

185
00:07:25.390 --> 00:07:29.390
The reason for that trip was multi-fold.

186
00:07:29.390 --> 00:07:33.500
I mean, it came about because Daniel,

187
00:07:33.500 --> 00:07:37.730
whom agreed to be on the podcast, that episode just went up.

188
00:07:37.730 --> 00:07:41.760
I got to dine at his extraordinary 11 Madison Park

189
00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:45.070
and followed up the next day, by doing the podcast with him.

190
00:07:45.070 --> 00:07:46.420
He was so delightful.

191
00:07:46.420 --> 00:07:49.310
And what an extraordinary privileged experience it was

192
00:07:49.310 --> 00:07:52.240
to enjoy his crazy cuisine.

193
00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:53.680
I mean, it was amazing.

194
00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:57.440
Not to, you know, repeat yourself on this podcast.

195
00:07:57.440 --> 00:07:59.897
Do you get into all the dishes and that podcast?

196
00:07:59.897 --> 00:08:01.388
'Cause I haven't listened to it yet.

197
00:08:01.388 --> 00:08:02.221
No.

198
00:08:02.221 --> 00:08:04.573
So give us some highlights, give us the highlights.

199
00:08:04.573 --> 00:08:05.543
I can't remember the names of all of them,

200
00:08:05.543 --> 00:08:07.200
they're so exotic.

201
00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:09.600
But basically, he converted this restaurant

202
00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:11.840
into a 100% plant-based menu.

203
00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:15.070
And these experiences are like five hours long.

204
00:08:15.070 --> 00:08:17.590
Like you go into the restaurant, they greet you by name.

205
00:08:17.590 --> 00:08:20.690
Like they curate this unbelievably personal experience

206
00:08:20.690 --> 00:08:22.943
where you feel really special.

207
00:08:24.490 --> 00:08:26.370
And then they just start bringing dishes out

208
00:08:26.370 --> 00:08:30.660
and the servers know all the backstory on everything

209
00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:31.660
and they can explain to you

210
00:08:31.660 --> 00:08:32.780
how these things are made.

211
00:08:32.780 --> 00:08:36.570
And it's all very elaborate and very unique.

212
00:08:36.570 --> 00:08:39.870
Like not just what you might expect with a plant-based menu,

213
00:08:39.870 --> 00:08:42.640
but stuff that's like very outside of, you know,

214
00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:44.700
very surprising in many ways.

215
00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:46.080
All the dishes are pretty small,

216
00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:48.520
but they bring so many that by the time you're done

217
00:08:48.520 --> 00:08:50.203
you're sated.
Right.

218
00:08:50.203 --> 00:08:52.030
But you're not feeling...

219
00:08:52.030 --> 00:08:54.580
Typically, if you go to like a super fancy restaurant,

220
00:08:54.580 --> 00:08:59.580
particularly that's of French influence, for example,

221
00:08:59.860 --> 00:09:01.320
the dishes tend to be really rich

222
00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:02.590
and you just feel very heavy

223
00:09:02.590 --> 00:09:04.280
and it's very meat laden.

224
00:09:04.280 --> 00:09:07.200
But here, you felt content,

225
00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:10.650
but also energetic, which was cool.

226
00:09:10.650 --> 00:09:13.200
They take everybody into the kitchen at one point,

227
00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:15.200
to give you this tour.
It's immaculate that kitchen

228
00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:16.630
I know, it's crazy.

229
00:09:16.630 --> 00:09:19.400
I was like, how do you keep this place so clean?

230
00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:24.400
With this huge portrait of Miles Davis in there,

231
00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:27.930
and like some words of creative inspiration

232
00:09:27.930 --> 00:09:29.690
and there's so many people working back there.

233
00:09:29.690 --> 00:09:31.380
It's this unbelievable machine,

234
00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:35.790
where Daniel has to be head chef, but also host.

235
00:09:35.790 --> 00:09:39.070
He has to share personal moments with every patron.

236
00:09:39.070 --> 00:09:42.050
It's quite a few hats that he has to wear.

237
00:09:42.050 --> 00:09:45.190
But it's a machine that's like super well-oiled

238
00:09:45.190 --> 00:09:46.978
and was pretty amazing.

239
00:09:46.978 --> 00:09:47.850
That's incredible.

240
00:09:47.850 --> 00:09:49.210
You went with Jeff Gordon, right?

241
00:09:49.210 --> 00:09:50.360
Yeah, exactly.

242
00:09:51.421 --> 00:09:54.660
Did he hook up that taste as you call him

243
00:09:54.660 --> 00:09:56.010
as soon as you to try to get in?

244
00:09:56.010 --> 00:09:58.210
Well, what happened was, when the news broke,

245
00:09:58.210 --> 00:09:59.440
and I've told this story before,

246
00:09:59.440 --> 00:10:00.450
so I don't wanna belabor it

247
00:10:00.450 --> 00:10:03.460
but, when the news broke that he was gonna be reopening

248
00:10:03.460 --> 00:10:05.360
the restaurant with a plant-based menu,

249
00:10:05.360 --> 00:10:06.960
the food world like exploded.

250
00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:08.350
Like I thought, oh, that's cool.

251
00:10:08.350 --> 00:10:11.490
I didn't realize the magnitude of that announcement,

252
00:10:11.490 --> 00:10:13.860
but the whole food world kind of lost their mind.

253
00:10:13.860 --> 00:10:15.040
Yeah.

254
00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:17.134
And Jeff texted me and he's like,

255
00:10:17.134 --> 00:10:18.750
"You got to get on this right away."

256
00:10:18.750 --> 00:10:20.780
And I was like, "Well, hook me up, that's cool."

257
00:10:20.780 --> 00:10:22.050
Like, let's do it.

258
00:10:22.050 --> 00:10:24.670
He connected the dots, Daniel got right back to me

259
00:10:24.670 --> 00:10:26.360
and we kind of went from there.

260
00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:29.220
That's cool.
So yeah, it was great.

261
00:10:29.220 --> 00:10:30.470
That's what anchored the trip.

262
00:10:30.470 --> 00:10:33.880
But I also was able to sit down with Eric Adams,

263
