All right, that's a good segue

1302
00:52:40.930 --> 00:52:42.690
to get into what we're gonna talk about next,

1303
00:52:42.690 --> 00:52:45.311
which is our summer watch list.

1304
00:52:45.311 --> 00:52:48.400
The must streams.
Yes, first annual Rich Roll

1305
00:52:48.400 --> 00:52:50.510
summer stream list, must streams.

1306
00:52:50.510 --> 00:52:53.360
We both made a lists of things that have captured

1307
00:52:53.360 --> 00:52:55.070
our fascination and attention

1308
00:52:55.070 --> 00:52:56.540
and things that we think are worthy

1309
00:52:56.540 --> 00:52:58.010
of you guys checking out.

1310
00:52:58.010 --> 00:52:59.070
Some of which you know,

1311
00:52:59.070 --> 00:53:00.640
are very much in the zeitgeists

1312
00:53:00.640 --> 00:53:01.810
and perhaps you've already seen,

1313
00:53:01.810 --> 00:53:04.220
some of which are a little bit off the beaten path,

1314
00:53:04.220 --> 00:53:09.220
but at the very top of my list is Bo Burnham's inside.

1315
00:53:09.400 --> 00:53:11.870
This thing haunts me.

1316
00:53:11.870 --> 00:53:14.030
Like I saw it maybe, I don't know,

1317
00:53:14.030 --> 00:53:14.910
three weeks ago.

1318
00:53:14.910 --> 00:53:16.830
When did I text you and said like, I...

1319
00:53:16.830 --> 00:53:19.170
It was right after we recorded last time.

1320
00:53:19.170 --> 00:53:20.711
It was like the weekend after.

1321
00:53:20.711 --> 00:53:22.470
I think it was about three or four weeks ago.

1322
00:53:22.470 --> 00:53:24.530
I can't stop thinking about.
Oh my God.

1323
00:53:24.530 --> 00:53:26.250
It's unbelievable.

1324
00:53:26.250 --> 00:53:29.580
I truly think this is a master work,

1325
00:53:29.580 --> 00:53:30.960
what this guy accomplished.

1326
00:53:30.960 --> 00:53:32.760
And for people that aren't familiar,

1327
00:53:32.760 --> 00:53:35.980
Bo Burnham is a stand-up comedian,

1328
00:53:35.980 --> 00:53:37.620
he's a film director,

1329
00:53:37.620 --> 00:53:40.020
he's a writer, he's done many things.

1330
00:53:40.020 --> 00:53:43.160
He's kind of a theater kid who grew up on the internet

1331
00:53:43.160 --> 00:53:45.960
and went viral as a young kid,

1332
00:53:45.960 --> 00:53:47.660
singing songs and telling jokes,

1333
00:53:47.660 --> 00:53:50.260
on YouTube and on Vine and has matured

1334
00:53:50.260 --> 00:53:54.210
into a pretty prominent talent in Hollywood.

1335
00:53:54.210 --> 00:53:56.960
He wrote and directed the movie eighth grade, which I loved.

1336
00:53:56.960 --> 00:54:01.190
I thought it was magical and a really special movie.

1337
00:54:01.190 --> 00:54:04.590
And he's done a number of comedy specials over the years,

1338
00:54:04.590 --> 00:54:06.700
but this comedy special,

1339
00:54:06.700 --> 00:54:09.010
and I hesitate to even call it a comedy special 'cause

1340
00:54:09.010 --> 00:54:12.660
I don't really think that that captures what exactly it is.

1341
00:54:12.660 --> 00:54:16.410
It's sort of a private concert film.

1342
00:54:16.410 --> 00:54:18.320
There is a comedy special component to it,

1343
00:54:18.320 --> 00:54:21.160
but it's performance art, it's isolation, theater

1344
00:54:21.160 --> 00:54:23.953
and it really is cinema.
But DIY cinema.

1345
00:54:24.905 --> 00:54:25.738
Very DIY.

1346
00:54:25.738 --> 00:54:28.070
He basically took the pandemic year

1347
00:54:28.070 --> 00:54:29.550
to create this special.

1348
00:54:29.550 --> 00:54:31.020
He did the entire thing

1349
00:54:31.020 --> 00:54:34.540
in his little guest house where he lit it,

1350
00:54:34.540 --> 00:54:37.610
he shot it, he wrote it, he performed it,

1351
00:54:37.610 --> 00:54:42.410
he edited it and created this really compelling

1352
00:54:42.410 --> 00:54:47.410
multilayered, profound piece of art that I don't know,

1353
00:54:52.840 --> 00:54:55.880
I'm at a loss to figuring out the language to describe it

1354
00:54:55.880 --> 00:54:58.260
because it is so unlike anything I've ever seen.

1355
00:54:58.260 --> 00:55:03.260
And I think it entertains and operates on so many levels.

1356
00:55:03.310 --> 00:55:07.460
And the reason why it haunts me and has stayed with me

1357
00:55:07.460 --> 00:55:12.170
is because of that multi-layered aspect to it.

1358
00:55:12.170 --> 00:55:14.800
Like you can watch it on its surface

1359
00:55:14.800 --> 00:55:15.880
and be entertained by it,

1360
00:55:15.880 --> 00:55:18.160
but the more you think about it, the more you realize,

1361
00:55:18.160 --> 00:55:23.160
like, how deep he's probing issues of isolation,

1362
00:55:25.580 --> 00:55:30.580
issues of internet culture, youth culture, depression,

1363
00:55:30.800 --> 00:55:35.070
loneliness, suicide, and you know,

1364
00:55:36.620 --> 00:55:39.488
the attention seeking that is part and parcel

1365
00:55:39.488 --> 00:55:43.670
of his generation, as somebody, I think he was born in 1990,

1366
00:55:43.670 --> 00:55:44.810
grew up on the internet.

1367
00:55:44.810 --> 00:55:47.060
The internet has always been,

1368
00:55:47.060 --> 00:55:49.280
you know, who he is and where he has gone

1369
00:55:49.280 --> 00:55:54.050
to perform and this interesting, difficult,

1370
00:55:54.050 --> 00:55:58.670
challenging relationship that he has with social media

1371
00:55:58.670 --> 00:56:00.530
in the sense that it birthed him

1372
00:56:00.530 --> 00:56:02.550
and has given him this outlet,

1373
00:56:02.550 --> 00:56:05.210
and yet it's also so destructive

1374
00:56:05.210 --> 00:56:08.140
and emotionally debilitating at the same time.

1375
00:56:08.140 --> 00:56:11.480
And he probes all of this stuff with songs,

1376
00:56:11.480 --> 00:56:14.297
with interludes, with, you know,

1377
00:56:15.810 --> 00:56:19.090
think pieces that are peppered throughout this whole thing.

1378
00:56:19.090 --> 00:56:23.087
And you see this trajectory from, you know,

1379
00:56:24.680 --> 00:56:26.120
it kinda opens and he's in a...

1380
00:56:26.120 --> 00:56:29.100
It's relatively light and gleeful as it begins

1381
00:56:29.100 --> 00:56:32.270
and there's a slow degradation of his mental wellbeing

1382
00:56:32.270 --> 00:56:35.060
throughout the course of the hour until, you know,

1383
00:56:35.060 --> 00:56:38.290
he's quite despondent at the end and has to find,

1384
00:56:38.290 --> 00:56:41.040
you know, some salvation.

1385
00:56:41.040 --> 00:56:44.660
But I think it's kind of like, you can sense

1386
00:56:44.660 --> 00:56:46.930
the darkness in it really early.

1387
00:56:46.930 --> 00:56:50.920
I mean, it is a very, to say it's very dark

1388
00:56:50.920 --> 00:56:52.330
is not to put you off.

1389
00:56:52.330 --> 00:56:54.570
It's dark, but in a way that great art

1390
00:56:54.570 --> 00:56:56.090
has to be in a way.

1391
00:56:56.090 --> 00:56:57.943
It's the most honest,

1392
00:56:58.930 --> 00:57:02.140
I think the best piece of satire for the internet age

1393
00:57:02.140 --> 00:57:03.223
that we have seen.

1394
00:57:04.690 --> 00:57:07.980
Nothing I think, that I've seen can even come close

1395
00:57:07.980 --> 00:57:09.960
to the commentary that's in this.

1396
00:57:09.960 --> 00:57:11.530
It just shows you how, you know,

1397
00:57:11.530 --> 00:57:16.530
this is like, who was the first comic to swear?

1398
00:57:17.510 --> 00:57:21.463
You know, like Lenny Bruce, like this is like to me,

1399
00:57:22.930 --> 00:57:27.430
for sure, all time, great comedy work of art.

1400
00:57:27.430 --> 00:57:30.030
You know, it's like, for of its age,

1401
00:57:30.030 --> 00:57:31.480
there's nothing that even comes close

1402
00:57:31.480 --> 00:57:33.150
to being able to push buttons like this

1403
00:57:33.150 --> 00:57:34.550
that I've seen in a long time.

1404
00:57:34.550 --> 00:57:37.270
It's such a trope to say voice of a generation.

1405
00:57:37.270 --> 00:57:39.040
You know, it's such a trite turn of phrase,

1406
00:57:39.040 --> 00:57:43.150
but I think it is apropos in his case

1407
00:57:43.150 --> 00:57:44.340
and the case of this work.

1408
00:57:44.340 --> 00:57:45.680
I mean, it's unbelievably,

1409
00:57:45.680 --> 00:57:50.170
self-referential it's meta and the level of self-awareness

1410
00:57:50.170 --> 00:57:51.130
that he brings to it.

1411
00:57:51.130 --> 00:57:54.710
Like the idea is that he is authentically performing

1412
00:57:54.710 --> 00:57:56.380
and bearing his vulnerable self

1413
00:57:56.380 --> 00:57:59.060
while also reviewing it at the same time.

1414
00:57:59.060 --> 00:58:01.730
So it folds in on itself.

1415
00:58:01.730 --> 00:58:03.780
And he's also letting you know that

1416
00:58:03.780 --> 00:58:05.980
he knows that at the same time.

1417
00:58:05.980 --> 00:58:10.210
So it begs the question of what is truly authentic?

1418
00:58:10.210 --> 00:58:13.580
Is this vulnerability true or is this a performance?

1419
00:58:13.580 --> 00:58:16.060
And you're not quite sure where that lies.

1420
00:58:16.060 --> 00:58:17.560
Like when he's breaking down,

1421
00:58:17.560 --> 00:58:20.080
is he really breaking down or is he winking at you

1422
00:58:20.080 --> 00:58:22.680
and saying, this is part of the performance as well.

1423
00:58:22.680 --> 00:58:23.940
Right.

1424
00:58:23.940 --> 00:58:26.690
I mean, as a performance art piece,

1425
00:58:26.690 --> 00:58:28.150
it doesn't even really matter.

1426
00:58:28.150 --> 00:58:31.170
Like there was some blowback I heard

1427
00:58:31.170 --> 00:58:32.727
that people were saying, "Oh, you know,

1428
00:58:32.727 --> 00:58:34.337
"he's trying to pretend that he's lived

1429
00:58:34.337 --> 00:58:35.933
"in that room for the whole year."

1430
00:58:35.933 --> 00:58:37.337
"And it's actually a guest house,

1431
00:58:37.337 --> 00:58:38.170
"really nice house."

1432
00:58:38.170 --> 00:58:40.367
"And he has a partner that he lives with

1433
00:58:40.367 --> 00:58:41.437
"and he's got a great life

1434
00:58:41.437 --> 00:58:42.407
"and he's not depressed like that."

1435
00:58:42.407 --> 00:58:43.780
And I'm like, well, first of all,

1436
00:58:43.780 --> 00:58:47.060
you can't fake that kind of introspection and a genius

1437
00:58:47.060 --> 00:58:50.450
and it usually does come with a very big dark side

1438
00:58:50.450 --> 00:58:51.820
and he grew up religious too.

1439
00:58:51.820 --> 00:58:54.780
So like, that is in there as well.

1440
00:58:54.780 --> 00:58:57.870
And, from what I read, I don't wanna speak for him,

1441
00:58:57.870 --> 00:58:59.020
but that's what I read.

1442
00:59:02.310 --> 00:59:03.940
So, to me that kind of criticism,

1443
00:59:03.940 --> 00:59:05.890
which is there's always going to be haters,

1444
00:59:05.890 --> 00:59:07.647
but I did hear there was pushback on him.

1445
00:59:07.647 --> 00:59:12.220
I'm like, this is a piece of art, like you know,

1446
00:59:12.220 --> 00:59:14.750
for you to try to say that it's like, it doesn't have...

1447
00:59:14.750 --> 00:59:16.580
But also that's part of the art,

1448
00:59:16.580 --> 00:59:18.223
that's part of the conversation.

1449
00:59:18.223 --> 00:59:21.713
Like that self-awareness extends to his understanding.

1450
00:59:23.771 --> 00:59:26.820
He knows that the audience knows that he has a partner,

1451
00:59:26.820 --> 00:59:29.770
who's a successful film director and that he probably,

1452
00:59:29.770 --> 00:59:32.820
you know, makes quite a bit of money doing what he does.

1453
00:59:32.820 --> 00:59:35.440
So is this performance, is it real?

1454
00:59:35.440 --> 00:59:36.620
And where is that line?

1455
00:59:36.620 --> 00:59:39.920
I mean, it almost doesn't matter.

1456
00:59:39.920 --> 00:59:42.900
The commentary remains, you know, significant,

1457
00:59:42.900 --> 00:59:46.910
I think, whether or not it's true

1458
00:59:46.910 --> 00:59:48.320
to the extent to which it's true,

1459
00:59:48.320 --> 00:59:49.230
but I think you're correct.

1460
00:59:49.230 --> 00:59:51.690
Like he was degrading, you know,

1461
00:59:51.690 --> 00:59:56.690
and it's this confluence or this overlap of analyzing

1462
00:59:58.760 --> 01:00:01.380
how the internet impacts all of us

1463
01:00:01.380 --> 01:00:03.550
in that self-referential way.

1464
01:00:03.550 --> 01:00:07.973
Him as observer, but also participant at the same time,

1465
01:00:10.539 --> 01:00:14.360
a commentary on the pandemic and the progressive isolation

1466
01:00:14.360 --> 01:00:16.650
and mental and emotional despair

1467
01:00:16.650 --> 01:00:18.940
that goes hand in hand with that.

1468
01:00:18.940 --> 01:00:23.930
Yeah, and then, you know, two songs,

1469
01:00:23.930 --> 01:00:27.130
one is, welcome to the internet, which starts

1470
01:00:27.130 --> 01:00:29.470
with a polka, welcome to the internet.

1471
01:00:29.470 --> 01:00:32.977
And he goes into this, you know, this quote you have here,

1472
01:00:32.977 --> 01:00:35.307
"could I interest you in everything all of the time,

1473
01:00:35.307 --> 01:00:37.417
"a little bit of everything, all of the time,

1474
01:00:37.417 --> 01:00:39.757
"apathy is a tragedy and boredom is a crime,

1475
01:00:39.757 --> 01:00:41.710
"anything and everything all of the time."

1476
01:00:41.710 --> 01:00:43.630
I mean, that song might be the single best

1477
01:00:43.630 --> 01:00:46.450
critique of the internet I've ever seen or heard.

1478
01:00:46.450 --> 01:00:48.943
And it goes on and on and on and it's amazing.

1479
01:00:50.140 --> 01:00:53.070
You can't say enough how great the songs are.

1480
01:00:53.070 --> 01:00:55.666
They're like ear worms and they're satire.

1481
01:00:55.666 --> 01:00:58.080
Like, you don't really tend to listen

1482
01:00:58.080 --> 01:01:01.620
to like comedy songs for fun,

1483
01:01:01.620 --> 01:01:03.280
but it's deeper than that.

1484
01:01:03.280 --> 01:01:05.910
And you know, whether it's that one

1485
01:01:05.910 --> 01:01:07.400
or white girl's Instagram.
The Bezos song.

1486
01:01:07.400 --> 01:01:08.930
I mean, white girl's Instagram is probably

1487
01:01:08.930 --> 01:01:10.300
the catchiest one.

1488
01:01:10.300 --> 01:01:13.533
I'm sure on YouTube, it's got insane views at this point.

1489
01:01:14.790 --> 01:01:15.791
I wanna sing them all.

1490
01:01:15.791 --> 01:01:18.960
He makes this music video of this song,

1491
01:01:18.960 --> 01:01:20.960
you know, white girl's Instagram,

1492
01:01:20.960 --> 01:01:22.830
that is just so on the nose.

1493
01:01:22.830 --> 01:01:24.511
It's amazing.
It's unbelievable.

1494
01:01:24.511 --> 01:01:27.630
And then he uses Auto-Tune,

1495
01:01:27.630 --> 01:01:30.240
which you know, is also part of his satire.

1496
01:01:30.240 --> 01:01:31.217
And what's amazing.

1497
01:01:31.217 --> 01:01:35.850
He did that onstage in another show that he did,

1498
01:01:35.850 --> 01:01:38.130
where he was sort of doing commentary

1499
01:01:38.130 --> 01:01:41.480
on Kanye and he started doing this Auto-Tune song

1500
01:01:41.480 --> 01:01:43.660
about why the Pringles jar wasn't wide enough

1501
01:01:43.660 --> 01:01:44.960
for your hand to go in it.

1502
01:01:46.210 --> 01:01:47.370
'Cause people on the internet are doing

1503
01:01:47.370 --> 01:01:49.123
auto-tuned stuff on loop.

1504
01:01:49.123 --> 01:01:51.748
And also he self flagellates himself

1505
01:01:51.748 --> 01:01:55.117
in this martyr, you know, this sort of satirical,

1506
01:01:55.117 --> 01:01:57.760
I'm a martyr white guy, like I'm aware like,

1507
01:01:57.760 --> 01:02:00.010
