2822
01:57:49.470 --> 01:57:51.330
Especially when you consider that, you know,

2823
01:57:51.330 --> 01:57:54.020
he basically was in this state where his career

2824
01:57:54.020 --> 01:57:55.950
was essentially over.

2825
01:57:55.950 --> 01:57:58.510
He hadn't won a stage in five years.

2826
01:57:58.510 --> 01:58:00.070
He hadn't won the green Jersey.

2827
01:58:00.070 --> 01:58:02.250
This is the second time that he's won the green jersy.

2828
01:58:02.250 --> 01:58:06.680
The first time was 10 years ago and he almost hung it up.

2829
01:58:06.680 --> 01:58:09.513
He got a one-year contract with this team.

2830
01:58:10.430 --> 01:58:12.140
He wasn't gonna go to the tour de France.

2831
01:58:12.140 --> 01:58:14.440
He wasn't training to go to the tour de France

2832
01:58:16.150 --> 01:58:19.240
and then their lead sprinter, Sam Bennett,

2833
01:58:19.240 --> 01:58:22.680
this Irish guy who was last year's green jersey winner

2834
01:58:22.680 --> 01:58:24.710
gets injured, can attend.

2835
01:58:24.710 --> 01:58:27.280
They elevate Cavendish to go to the tour.

2836
01:58:27.280 --> 01:58:29.770
There was zero expectations of this guy, you know,

2837
01:58:29.770 --> 01:58:31.380
doing anything significant.

2838
01:58:31.380 --> 01:58:33.880
And he ends up winning four stages,

2839
01:58:33.880 --> 01:58:35.550
runs away with a green Jersey.

2840
01:58:35.550 --> 01:58:36.870
I mean, it's crazy.

2841
01:58:36.870 --> 01:58:39.220
And over the past couple of years,

2842
01:58:39.220 --> 01:58:40.900
he's dealt with Epstein-Barr Virus.

2843
01:58:40.900 --> 01:58:42.180
He's had several crashes.

2844
01:58:42.180 --> 01:58:44.320
There's been some depression,

2845
01:58:44.320 --> 01:58:48.460
all kinds of stuff that would indicate that

2846
01:58:49.540 --> 01:58:53.080
his days of being in top form were in the rear view mirror.

2847
01:58:53.080 --> 01:58:55.540
So how can you not love that story?

2848
01:58:55.540 --> 01:58:57.860
It's unbelievable.
Unbelievable.

2849
01:58:57.860 --> 01:58:58.720
A little bit of drama,

2850
01:58:58.720 --> 01:59:00.900
he threw a tantrum a couple of days ago

2851
01:59:00.900 --> 01:59:03.010
in front of a bike mechanic.

2852
01:59:03.010 --> 01:59:06.060
People lost their minds a little bit on Twitter.

2853
01:59:06.060 --> 01:59:07.070
There's a lot of bagging on him,

2854
01:59:07.070 --> 01:59:10.930
but listen, man, you don't win the green jersey

2855
01:59:10.930 --> 01:59:12.740
a second time and win four stages

2856
01:59:12.740 --> 01:59:14.630
unless you're a bit of a perfectionist.

2857
01:59:14.630 --> 01:59:17.610
And so he had a little bit of a hot head moment.

2858
01:59:17.610 --> 01:59:19.010
Who cares.
Get over it.

2859
01:59:20.375 --> 01:59:21.660
He's always been on Instagram.

2860
01:59:21.660 --> 01:59:23.540
He's always talking about his teammates

2861
01:59:23.540 --> 01:59:24.470
and how much he loves them

2862
01:59:24.470 --> 01:59:26.960
and how grateful he is for all their support.

2863
01:59:26.960 --> 01:59:29.410
So I think he just had had a moment.

2864
01:59:29.410 --> 01:59:31.470
You're allowed to have moments in life.

2865
01:59:31.470 --> 01:59:34.520
I mean, we have to allow people

2866
01:59:34.520 --> 01:59:37.090
to be not nice sometimes, it's okay.

2867
01:59:37.090 --> 01:59:39.690
He regretted it, he apologized.

2868
01:59:39.690 --> 01:59:41.473
I think that apology was genuine.

2869
01:59:42.327 --> 01:59:43.670
You know these guys are exhausted.

2870
01:59:43.670 --> 01:59:45.421
We're not talking about him because he's the nicest guy

2871
01:59:45.421 --> 01:59:47.460
in town, we're talking about him because he did

2872
01:59:47.460 --> 01:59:49.330
something only Eddy Merck had done before.

2873
01:59:49.330 --> 01:59:50.690
Right, exactly

2874
01:59:50.690 --> 01:59:53.210
Speaking of doing something only one other person

2875
01:59:53.210 --> 01:59:54.950
had ever done before.

2876
01:59:54.950 --> 01:59:57.350
My favorite kind of emergent talent

2877
01:59:57.350 --> 02:00:00.140
out of the tour is Out Van Aert,

2878
02:00:00.140 --> 02:00:01.170
this guy's unbelievable.

2879
02:00:01.170 --> 02:00:05.430
26 years old, not a newcomer to the tour de France,

2880
02:00:05.430 --> 02:00:06.520
he's been around for a little bit,

2881
02:00:06.520 --> 02:00:09.160
but he won a sprint stage.

2882
02:00:09.160 --> 02:00:10.660
He almost won a second sprint stage.

2883
02:00:10.660 --> 02:00:12.750
He almost out sprinted Cav and another sprint stage.

2884
02:00:12.750 --> 02:00:14.590
He almost took him to sprint stages.

2885
02:00:14.590 --> 02:00:17.383
But he also won a mountain stage,

2886
02:00:19.760 --> 02:00:20.720
a climbing stage where

2887
02:00:20.720 --> 02:00:23.490
he broke away from the pack and he won

2888
02:00:23.490 --> 02:00:27.950
the individual time trial, which is like unbelievable.

2889
02:00:27.950 --> 02:00:30.010
The versatility of this guy.

2890
02:00:30.010 --> 02:00:32.350
So he won the final sprint stage on the final day

2891
02:00:32.350 --> 02:00:37.330
on the Champs-Elysees beating Cav.

2892
02:00:37.330 --> 02:00:41.420
Nobody has won a sprint climbing and time trial stage

2893
02:00:41.420 --> 02:00:43.440
since Bernardino in 1979.

2894
02:00:43.440 --> 02:00:47.390
So to me, that's super exciting to watch him.

2895
02:00:47.390 --> 02:00:51.660
And of course we have Tadej Pogacar are on a second win.

2896
02:00:51.660 --> 02:00:53.810
This kid's only 22 years old,

2897
02:00:53.810 --> 02:00:56.650
second yellow Jersey from Slovenia.

2898
02:00:56.650 --> 02:00:58.280
They make them hard in Slovenia,

2899
02:00:58.280 --> 02:01:01.030
I can tell you there's a lot of crazy ultra endurance

2900
02:01:01.030 --> 02:01:03.200
athletes, exceptional ultra endurance

2901
02:01:03.200 --> 02:01:04.700
athletes from Slovenia.

2902
02:01:04.700 --> 02:01:06.390
There's another Slovenian we're going to talk about

2903
02:01:06.390 --> 02:01:07.520
in a little bit.

2904
02:01:07.520 --> 02:01:10.500
There is isn't there, before we do that.

2905
02:01:10.500 --> 02:01:14.200
Yeah, so this guy, yellow jersey, but also the white jersey,

2906
02:01:14.200 --> 02:01:17.020
'cause he's the outstanding young rider

2907
02:01:17.020 --> 02:01:18.480
and the polka dot jersey winner.

2908
02:01:18.480 --> 02:01:21.140
I mean, where does this guy go from here.

2909
02:01:21.140 --> 02:01:22.250
To a bigger closet.

2910
02:01:22.250 --> 02:01:25.393
He's gotta get one of those Home Depot closet installers.

2911
02:01:27.210 --> 02:01:29.700
He needs a bigger closet 'cause the closet space

2912
02:01:29.700 --> 02:01:31.110
in Europe is smaller.

2913
02:01:31.110 --> 02:01:33.060
Okay, I don't know what you're talking about.

2914
02:01:33.060 --> 02:01:34.950
You said where he goes from here.

2915
02:01:34.950 --> 02:01:35.940
He's got all these jerseys.

2916
02:01:35.940 --> 02:01:37.260
He does, yeah.

2917
02:01:37.260 --> 02:01:40.290
I'm sure I'll hang them on his wall or do something.

2918
02:01:40.290 --> 02:01:41.750
But unbelievable performance.

2919
02:01:41.750 --> 02:01:43.520
He was never really challenged at all

2920
02:01:43.520 --> 02:01:44.850
throughout this whole thing.

2921
02:01:44.850 --> 02:01:49.580
So he's set up.
A long career.

2922
02:01:49.580 --> 02:01:53.110
He set up for potentially a couple more of these.

2923
02:01:53.110 --> 02:01:55.780
Legend, legendary status.

2924
02:01:55.780 --> 02:01:58.430
I did wanna shout out, the announcers,

2925
02:01:58.430 --> 02:01:59.760
like we're unplugged at our house,

2926
02:01:59.760 --> 02:02:01.600
so I don't have television.

2927
02:02:01.600 --> 02:02:04.510
I don't even know if I have like an NBC streaming.

2928
02:02:04.510 --> 02:02:06.550
I don't have a peacock or anything like that.

2929
02:02:06.550 --> 02:02:08.530
But when I was in New York City, like I said,

2930
02:02:08.530 --> 02:02:11.230
I would go home every night and watch the tour

2931
02:02:11.230 --> 02:02:12.850
and went into that thinking,

2932
02:02:12.850 --> 02:02:14.320
how exciting is this tour gonna be?

2933
02:02:14.320 --> 02:02:18.210
I hadn't really been following a cycling that closely,

2934
02:02:18.210 --> 02:02:20.710
but I thought it was a super exciting tour

2935
02:02:20.710 --> 02:02:23.420
and I think that the commentators

2936
02:02:26.401 --> 02:02:28.060
deserve a lot of credit for that.

2937
02:02:28.060 --> 02:02:31.100
Again, Phil Ligget being the goat,

2938
02:02:31.100 --> 02:02:34.690
but right next to him, Bob Roll, my namesake.

2939
02:02:34.690 --> 02:02:36.230
You know how many times I get discounts?

2940
02:02:36.230 --> 02:02:37.277
I go into bike shops.
Really?

2941
02:02:37.277 --> 02:02:40.670
When I go into a bike shop and I need to buy something,

2942
02:02:40.670 --> 02:02:43.970
invariably, they're like, are you related to Bob Roll?

2943
02:02:43.970 --> 02:02:44.890
And you say, yes.

2944
02:02:44.890 --> 02:02:46.665
I say yes, and I get a discount.

2945
02:02:46.665 --> 02:02:48.748
(laughs)

2946
02:02:49.910 --> 02:02:52.130
Paul Burmeister, who I was not familiar with,

2947
02:02:52.130 --> 02:02:55.350
but also Christian Vanderbilt, shout out to Christian.

2948
02:02:55.350 --> 02:02:57.900
Those guys did an unbelievable job.

2949
02:02:57.900 --> 02:03:00.780
So thank you for riveting me.

2950
02:03:00.780 --> 02:03:02.330
What else we got to talk about?

2951
02:03:03.700 --> 02:03:06.690
Well, we have Hardrock 100.

2952
02:03:06.690 --> 02:03:07.523
Yes.

2953
02:03:08.820 --> 02:03:10.780
Hardrock 100, a hundred miles.

2954
02:03:10.780 --> 02:03:13.529
We're turning into an endurance report card here

2955
02:03:13.529 --> 02:03:14.460
with everything that's going on.

2956
02:03:14.460 --> 02:03:17.590
But yeah, Hardrock 100 was this past weekend.

2957
02:03:17.590 --> 02:03:21.940
This race, I can't wrap my head around how difficult

2958
02:03:21.940 --> 02:03:22.773
this race must be.

2959
02:03:22.773 --> 02:03:27.020
It's a hundred mile foot race with 30,000 feet

2960
02:03:27.020 --> 02:03:31.160
of elevation gain at 11,000 feet of altitude.

2961
02:03:31.160 --> 02:03:33.930
And they haven't run this race in the past couple of years.

2962
02:03:33.930 --> 02:03:37.640
In 2019, there was snow and some risk of avalanche,

2963
02:03:37.640 --> 02:03:38.473
so they canceled it.

2964
02:03:38.473 --> 02:03:43.473
In 2020, of course COVID and it ended that from happening.

2965
02:03:43.650 --> 02:03:47.290
So it hasn't happened in a little while,

2966
02:03:47.290 --> 02:03:49.530
but it was a pretty exciting race this year.

2967
02:03:49.530 --> 02:03:52.740
The top three men all broke the previous counterclockwise

2968
02:03:52.740 --> 02:03:54.810
record of 23 hours and 28 minutes

2969
02:03:54.810 --> 02:03:58.430
set by Kilian Jornet in 2015.

2970
02:03:58.430 --> 02:04:01.330
In first place this guy Francoise D'Haene finished

2971
02:04:01.330 --> 02:04:05.760
in 21 hours and 45 minutes, which is the fastest time ever.

2972
02:04:05.760 --> 02:04:08.430
I guess the clockwise version of the race they alternate

2973
02:04:08.430 --> 02:04:10.620
year by year is a little bit easier.

2974
02:04:10.620 --> 02:04:13.423
And Kilian had that record at 2241.

2975
02:04:14.890 --> 02:04:16.850
It's a huge accomplishment when anyone

2976
02:04:16.850 --> 02:04:19.720
runs a hundred miles in under 24 hours

2977
02:04:19.720 --> 02:04:24.670
and these guys are getting 33,000 feet of elevation gain,

2978
02:04:24.670 --> 02:04:27.490
and this guy does it in 21 hours and 45 minutes.

2979
02:04:27.490 --> 02:04:29.743
Like it just bends my mind.

2980
02:04:31.190 --> 02:04:36.190
And second place went to Dylan Bowman podcaster,

2981
02:04:36.360 --> 02:04:38.700
podcast fan, I think, what's up Dylan?

2982
02:04:38.700 --> 02:04:40.330
22 hours and 45 minutes.

2983
02:04:40.330 --> 02:04:41.810
He'd never done that race before.

2984
02:04:41.810 --> 02:04:45.640
So that's a huge accomplishment for Dylan

2985
02:04:45.640 --> 02:04:47.360
and super cool to see.

2986
02:04:47.360 --> 02:04:50.770
Unfortunately, Courtney Dauwalter,

2987
02:04:50.770 --> 02:04:53.480
that podcast is going up August 2nd, I think,

2988
02:04:53.480 --> 02:04:55.994
we recorded it a while ago.

2989
02:04:55.994 --> 02:04:58.270
Hardrock was one of two races that Courtney

2990
02:04:58.270 --> 02:05:01.010
was really keying in on for this year.

2991
02:05:01.010 --> 02:05:02.860
She was running in fourth place overall,

2992
02:05:02.860 --> 02:05:06.780
so Dylan Bowman, Ryan Smith and Francoise

2993
02:05:06.780 --> 02:05:08.790
were the only ones ahead of her.

2994
02:05:08.790 --> 02:05:10.370
But started having stomach issues

2995
02:05:10.370 --> 02:05:12.720
and at 62 miles, she dropped out.

2996
02:05:12.720 --> 02:05:15.290
So that's a bummer for her.

2997
02:05:15.290 --> 02:05:16.648
So I hated to see that.

2998
02:05:16.648 --> 02:05:18.500
You can't eat or drink
You're done.

2999
02:05:18.500 --> 02:05:19.792
You're done.

3000
02:05:19.792 --> 02:05:21.020
I think stomach issues turned into some

3001
02:05:21.020 --> 02:05:23.050
other kinds of issues and it was just the right thing

3002
02:05:23.050 --> 02:05:23.883
to pull out.

3003
02:05:23.883 --> 02:05:25.190
She's running UTMB in August,

3004
02:05:25.190 --> 02:05:28.410
so back to the drawing board and hopefully she's healthy

3005
02:05:28.410 --> 02:05:30.990
and figures all that stuff out

3006
02:05:30.990 --> 02:05:32.870
so that she can crush it there.

3007
02:05:32.870 --> 02:05:34.280
But the woman who won, this woman,

3008
02:05:34.280 --> 02:05:36.950
Sabrina Stanley she's the returning champ,

3009
02:05:36.950 --> 02:05:39.320
did it in 27 hours and 21 minutes.

3010
02:05:39.320 --> 02:05:41.360
Like it's just mind bending to me,

3011
02:05:41.360 --> 02:05:43.760
like at this kind of altitude and this kind of elevation

3012
02:05:43.760 --> 02:05:46.820
gain that these people can run that fast.

3013
02:05:46.820 --> 02:05:49.250
So anyway, that's that.

3014
02:05:49.250 --> 02:05:51.240
That's cool, I wanted to update everybody

3015
02:05:51.240 --> 02:05:53.510
on Jason Caldwell, we talked several weeks ago

3016
02:05:53.510 --> 02:05:56.140
about the great Pacific race.

3017
02:05:56.140 --> 02:05:58.240
Jason and his teammates, Angus Collins,

3018
02:05:58.240 --> 02:06:01.490
Gus Barton and Dunkin Roy who were attempting to break

3019
02:06:01.490 --> 02:06:04.820
the record for, how do you call it?

3020
02:06:04.820 --> 02:06:09.820
Like, the fastest ever to row from San Francisco to Hawaii,

3021
02:06:11.030 --> 02:06:12.900
which I guess is the Guinness record

3022
02:06:12.900 --> 02:06:16.090
for human powered crossing of the mid Pacific.

3023
02:06:16.090 --> 02:06:17.500
They completed it.

3024
02:06:17.500 --> 02:06:19.280
It's 2,400 nautical miles.

3025
02:06:19.280 --> 02:06:21.280
Only 50 have attempted this.

3026
02:06:21.280 --> 02:06:23.340
They met all kinds of weather and huge swells

3027
02:06:23.340 --> 02:06:25.050
and all that kind of stuff, but they broke the record

3028
02:06:25.050 --> 02:06:27.890
by nine days, which is insane.

3029
02:06:27.890 --> 02:06:30.230
Unbelievable, I was following that story with you

3030
02:06:30.230 --> 02:06:34.660
and I mean, that's just incredible, nine day.

3031
02:06:34.660 --> 02:06:37.433
Yeah.
Record shatter.

3032
02:06:38.946 --> 02:06:40.530
How long were they out there?

3033
02:06:40.530 --> 02:06:44.010
Let's see, they started May the 31st then they finished.

3034
02:06:44.010 --> 02:06:44.860
What day did they finish?

3035
02:06:44.860 --> 02:06:46.850
I don't know the cumulative time.

3036
02:06:46.850 --> 02:06:48.740
They've been done for a little while.

3037
02:06:48.740 --> 02:06:51.220
So, it was like 28 days or something like that.

3038
02:06:51.220 --> 02:06:53.630
I saw an interview with Jason and he was saying that

3039
02:06:53.630 --> 02:06:57.052
the big challenge immediately was that they can't walk.

3040
02:06:57.052 --> 02:06:59.270
Like literally, other than walking

3041
02:06:59.270 --> 02:07:00.590
from one end of the boat to the other,

3042
02:07:00.590 --> 02:07:02.250
they haven't walked in in a month.

3043
02:07:02.250 --> 02:07:03.280
Jelly legs.
They'll have to figure out

3044
02:07:03.280 --> 02:07:05.780
how to walk and there's some kind of weird atrophy

3045
02:07:05.780 --> 02:07:07.180
and all the weight loss that they experience.

3046
02:07:07.180 --> 02:07:08.619
Is that right?
Yeah.

3047
02:07:08.619 --> 02:07:11.493
They're like just torsos on twig legs.

3048
02:07:11.493 --> 02:07:12.326
(laughs)

3049
02:07:12.326 --> 02:07:13.200
I don't know.

3050
02:07:13.200 --> 02:07:16.080
But rowing requires a lot of leg strength too.

3051
02:07:16.080 --> 02:07:17.460
So I don't know how that works.

3052
02:07:17.460 --> 02:07:19.340
So maybe different types of muscles.

3053
02:07:19.340 --> 02:07:24.340
We can get them like one of those sit-down bicycles,

3054
02:07:26.920 --> 02:07:28.360
they can get around that way.

3055
02:07:28.360 --> 02:07:29.610
A recumbent.

3056
02:07:29.610 --> 02:07:31.160
Yeah the recumbent bicycle.

3057
02:07:32.130 --> 02:07:33.810
I can see you on a recumbent.

3058
02:07:33.810 --> 02:07:36.093
No you can't.
Going up and down PCH.

3059
02:07:37.697 --> 02:07:40.810
Grow some kind of weird beard and the little

3060
02:07:40.810 --> 02:07:42.210
you know, rear view mirror.

3061
02:07:42.210 --> 02:07:46.800
If this experiment, renting, the family house goes awry,

3062
02:07:46.800 --> 02:07:48.350
I could escape on a recumbent.

3063
02:07:48.350 --> 02:07:50.560
The recumbent bicycle is a close cousin

3064
02:07:50.560 --> 02:07:52.271
to the snorkel mask.

3065
02:07:52.271 --> 02:07:56.468
It's the cycling version of swimming snorkel mask.

3066
02:07:56.468 --> 02:07:58.635
(laughs)

3067
02:08:01.320 --> 02:08:02.970
Not in my world by friend.

3068
02:08:02.970 --> 02:08:06.250
So like cyclists look at recumbent cyclists,

3069
02:08:06.250 --> 02:08:08.910
the way swimmers look at people that were snorkel mask.

3070
02:08:08.910 --> 02:08:11.970
Maybe that's help You understand where I'm coming from.

3071
02:08:11.970 --> 02:08:13.230
I'm bringing the mask that Choe's house.

3072
02:08:13.230 --> 02:08:16.020
I didn't plan that, it came back very organically.

3073
02:08:16.020 --> 02:08:17.220
Good job.

3074
02:08:17.220 --> 02:08:18.313
I'm glad it's back.

3075
02:08:19.530 --> 02:08:21.230
The audience demands it.

3076
02:08:21.230 --> 02:08:23.643
Speaking of my swim mask,

